    "questions": {
      "type": "section",
      "blocks": {
        "faq_line": {
          "type": "group",
          "name": "Line",
          "settings": {
            "content_direction": "column",
            "gap": 0,
            "width": "fill",
            "width_mobile": "fill",
            "border": "solid",
            "border_width": 1,
            "border_opacity": 15,
            "border_color": ""
          },
          "blocks": {}
        },
        "faq_title": {
          "type": "text",
          "settings": {
            "text": "<h2>FAQs</h2>",
            "width": "100%",
            "max_width": "normal",
            "alignment": "left",
            "type_preset": "h3"
          },
          "blocks": {}
        },
        "faq_acc": {
          "type": "accordion",
          "name": "FAQ",
          "settings": {
            "icon": "plus",
            "dividers": true,
            "type_preset": "h6",
            "border": "none"
          },
          "blocks": {
            "faq_1": {
              "type": "_accordion-row",
              "settings": {
                "heading": "What comes in the kit?",
                "open_by_default": false,
                "icon": "none"
              },
              "blocks": {
                "faq_1_a": {
                  "type": "text",
                  "settings": {
                    "text": "<p>The $99 Wellington kit includes a silicone mold, length-adjusting bar, offset spatula and 50 g of Activa RM. The $299 full Wellington bundle adds a Predictive Thermometer and Kitchen Display, saving $29 compared with buying the kit and $229 thermometer-and-display set separately. Other recipe ingredients are not included.</p>",
                    "width": "100%",
                    "max_width": "none",
                    "alignment": "left",
                    "type_preset": "rte"
                  },
                  "blocks": {}
                }
              },
              "block_order": [
                "faq_1_a"
              ]
            },
            "faq_2": {
              "type": "_accordion-row",
              "settings": {
                "heading": "What size roast fits the mold?",
                "open_by_default": false,
                "icon": "none"
              },
              "blocks": {
                "faq_2_a": {
                  "type": "text",
                  "settings": {
                    "text": "<p>The 40 × 20 cm working area (about 15.7 × 7.9 in) is designed for a trimmed center-cut roast up to 20 cm long and about 7–8 cm in diameter before adding the layers. The 40 cm direction wraps around the roast; the 20 cm width runs along it. Allow for the layers and an overlap at the seam. A whole, untrimmed tenderloin may be too long. The mold measures 41 × 21 × 1 cm including the frame.</p>",
                    "width": "100%",
                    "max_width": "none",
                    "alignment": "left",
                    "type_preset": "rte"
                  },
                  "blocks": {}
                }
              },
              "block_order": [
                "faq_2_a"
              ]
            },
            "faq_3": {
              "type": "_accordion-row",
              "settings": {
                "heading": "How do I use the length-adjusting bar?",
                "open_by_default": false,
                "icon": "none"
              },
              "blocks": {
                "faq_3_a": {
                  "type": "text",
                  "settings": {
                    "text": "<p>Fit its patterned underside over a row of diamonds to make a shorter sheet for a slimmer roast. Fill between the bar and one end of the mold, leaving the other side empty. Check that the sheet will go around the roast and its layers, with an overlap at the seam. The width stays 20 cm.</p>",
                    "width": "100%",
                    "max_width": "none",
                    "alignment": "left",
                    "type_preset": "rte"
                  },
                  "blocks": {}
                }
              },
              "block_order": [
                "faq_3_a"
              ]
            },
            "faq_4": {
              "type": "_accordion-row",
              "settings": {
                "heading": "How does the mold make the lattice?",
                "open_by_default": false,
                "icon": "none"
              },
              "blocks": {
                "faq_4_a": {
                  "type": "text",
                  "settings": {
                    "text": "<p>The grooves cast the lattice pattern into a brioche-and-chicken crust. Filling to the rim creates a continuous backing above the diamonds, so the crust releases as one sheet. Spinach and mushroom duxelles farces form the next two layers.</p>",
                    "width": "100%",
                    "max_width": "none",
                    "alignment": "left",
                    "type_preset": "rte"
                  },
                  "blocks": {}
                }
              },
              "block_order": [
                "faq_4_a"
              ]
            },
            "faq_5": {
              "type": "_accordion-row",
              "settings": {
                "heading": "How do I use the mold to roll the Wellington?",
                "open_by_default": false,
                "icon": "none"
              },
              "blocks": {
                "faq_5_a": {
                  "type": "text",
                  "settings": {
                    "text": "<p>Keep the sheet in the mold while you bake and cool the brioche and spinach, then add Activa, uncooked duxelles farce, a second dusting of Activa and the raw tenderloin. Lift the silicone to help roll the sheet around the beef, peeling the mold away as you go. Leave both cut ends uncovered. Transfer the released roll onto plastic wrap, roll it tightly with the seam underneath and refrigerate overnight.</p>",
                    "width": "100%",
                    "max_width": "none",
                    "alignment": "left",
                    "type_preset": "rte"
                  },
                  "blocks": {}
                }
              },
              "block_order": [
                "faq_5_a"
              ]
            },
            "faq_6": {
              "type": "_accordion-row",
              "settings": {
                "heading": "What is the mold made from, and how do I care for it?",
                "open_by_default": false,
                "icon": "none"
              },
              "blocks": {
                "faq_6_a": {
                  "type": "text",
                  "settings": {
                    "text": "<p>The mold and bar are platinum-cured, food-grade silicone, specified for oven use up to 230°C / 446°F. Support the mold on a flat tray when filling or moving it. Wash with warm soapy water, rinse and dry. Avoid knives, abrasive scrubbers and direct contact with heating elements. Store flat or loosely rolled with the bar.</p>",
                    "width": "100%",
                    "max_width": "none",
                    "alignment": "left",
                    "type_preset": "rte"
                  },
                  "blocks": {}
                }
              },
              "block_order": [
                "faq_6_a"
              ]
            },
            "faq_7": {
              "type": "_accordion-row",
              "settings": {
                "heading": "What else can I use the offset spatula for?",
                "open_by_default": false,
                "icon": "none"
              },
              "blocks": {
                "faq_7_a": {
                  "type": "text",
                  "settings": {
                    "text": "<p>Frost a cake, smooth ganache or lift cookies and pastries. The angled neck keeps your fingers clear while the blade spreads and lifts. Use gentle pressure on silicone.</p>",
                    "width": "100%",
                    "max_width": "none",
                    "alignment": "left",
                    "type_preset": "rte"
                  },
                  "blocks": {}
                }
              },
              "block_order": [
                "faq_7_a"
              ]
            },
            "faq_8": {
              "type": "_accordion-row",
              "settings": {
                "heading": "What is Activa RM?",
                "open_by_default": false,
                "icon": "none"
              },
              "blocks": {
                "faq_8_a": {
                  "type": "text",
                  "settings": {
                    "text": "<p>Activa RM is a transglutaminase preparation from Ajinomoto, often called “meat glue.” It bonds proteins, helping the layered wrap hold against the beef.</p>",
                    "width": "100%",
                    "max_width": "none",
                    "alignment": "left",
                    "type_preset": "rte"
                  },
                  "blocks": {}
                }
              },
              "block_order": [
                "faq_8_a"
              ]
            },
            "faq_9": {
              "type": "_accordion-row",
              "settings": {
                "heading": "How do I apply Activa RM, and how long does bonding take?",
                "open_by_default": false,
                "icon": "none"
              },
              "blocks": {
                "faq_9_a": {
                  "type": "text",
                  "settings": {
                    "text": "<p>For this Wellington, use two light, even dustings: one on the cooled spinach before spreading the uncooked duxelles farce, and one on the duxelles before adding the beef. Use the recipe’s measured amount across both applications. Roll promptly, then tighten in plastic wrap to keep the layers in contact.</p><p>Refrigerate overnight for bonding. Ajinomoto’s general guidance for Activa RM is 4–24 hours under refrigeration; the required time varies with the food and conditions.</p><p>Ajinomoto’s general dosage guidance is 0.75–1% of the total mixture’s weight (7.5–10 g per kilogram), but the amount depends on the application. The 50 g pouch is not a single dose.</p>",
                    "width": "100%",
                    "max_width": "none",
                    "alignment": "left",
                    "type_preset": "rte"
                  },
                  "blocks": {}
                }
              },
              "block_order": [
                "faq_9_a"
              ]
            },
            "faq_10": {
              "type": "_accordion-row",
              "settings": {
                "heading": "How do I store and handle Activa RM?",
                "open_by_default": false,
                "icon": "none"
              },
              "blocks": {
                "faq_10_a": {
                  "type": "text",
                  "settings": {
                    "text": "<p>Keep it dry, minimize exposure to air and reseal promptly. Freeze after opening and portion with a clean, dry utensil. Ajinomoto recommends using opened RM within one month for best quality. Follow the date on your pouch; repacking does not restart its shelf life.</p><p>Avoid breathing powder dust. Keep raw ingredients cold and follow the recipe’s cooking instructions. Activa bonds proteins; it does not preserve food or replace cooking.</p>",
                    "width": "100%",
                    "max_width": "none",
                    "alignment": "left",
                    "type_preset": "rte"
                  },
                  "blocks": {}
                }
              },
              "block_order": [
                "faq_10_a"
              ]
            },
            "faq_11": {
              "type": "_accordion-row",
              "settings": {
                "heading": "Does Activa RM contain allergens?",
                "open_by_default": false,
                "icon": "none"
              },
              "blocks": {
                "faq_11_a": {
                  "type": "text",
                  "settings": {
                    "text": "<p><strong>Contains milk.</strong> Its ingredients are sodium caseinate (a milk protein), maltodextrin and transglutaminase. Activa RM is not suitable for a milk-free or vegan recipe.</p>",
                    "width": "100%",
                    "max_width": "none",
                    "alignment": "left",
                    "type_preset": "rte"
                  },
                  "blocks": {}
                }
              },
              "block_order": [
                "faq_11_a"
              ]
            },
            "faq_12": {
              "type": "_accordion-row",
              "settings": {
                "heading": "Are the images photographs of the product?",
                "open_by_default": false,
                "icon": "none"
              },
              "blocks": {
                "faq_12_a": {
                  "type": "text",
                  "settings": {
                    "text": "<p>The food and process images are illustrations, and the mold-kit images are digital renders. Thermometer and display photos are from Combustion. Actual color and appearance may vary.</p>",
                    "width": "100%",
                    "max_width": "none",
                    "alignment": "left",
                    "type_preset": "rte"
                  },
                  "blocks": {}
                }
              },
              "block_order": [
                "faq_12_a"
              ]
            }
          },
          "block_order": [
            "faq_1",
            "faq_2",
            "faq_3",
            "faq_4",
            "faq_5",
            "faq_6",
            "faq_7",
